    "payload": {
      "content": "When reviewing the ORIG dimension for papers analyzing existing methods (e.g., BYOL, SimSiam), prioritize the novelty of the theoretical lens (e.g., eigenspace analysis) and the resulting insights (e.g., implicit variance regularization) over the introduction of new tasks. A strong ORIG score is justified if the analysis reveals a mechanism that bridges different method families (e.g., asymmetric losses vs. covariance regularization) and leads to a methodological improvement (e.g., isotropic loss) that is well-justified by the theory. Experimental originality in such contexts is often neutral if standard benchmarks are used, provided the evaluation protocol specifically tests the theoretical claims (e.g., removing EMA).",
